  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/034e2813b404338a1b8d0ecfdb509781f7917e356155474d788d8bd85df0f221.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-2.$",
  "worked_solution": "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true, and I and III are false.",
